---
Title: Grouped endnotes
---

# Grouped endnotes

By default every inline footnote in a document collects into a single *Endnotes*
section appended at the very end — see [Footnotes](footnotes.html) for the note
syntax itself. That is the right behaviour for a short piece, but a longer
document often wants its notes **organised** and **placed**: a separate list of
notes after each section, endnotes gathered per chapter, a "Sources" list in one
spot and an "Editorial notes" list in another.

Grouped endnotes add exactly that, on top of the ordinary footnote syntax. You
give notes a **group** and then decide **where** each group's list appears. Two
small additions do the whole job — a way to label a note's group, and a marker
that says "put this group's list here". Everything on the [Footnotes](footnotes.html)
page still applies: anonymous versus labelled notes, multi-paragraph bodies, the
bracket-aware body scanner. This page only adds grouping and placement, and it is
purely additive — a document that uses none of it renders exactly as before.

## Assigning a note to a group

There are two ways to put a note in a group, and a note's group is resolved from
the **most specific** one that applies.

The direct way is to name the group **in the note**, in parentheses between the
marker and the colon. This works for both the labelled and the anonymous forms:

```
…a claim[^src: from Descartes]…            — default group (ungrouped)
…a claim[^src(refs): from Descartes]…      — labelled note in group "refs"
…a claim[fn(refs): from Descartes]…        — anonymous note in group "refs"
```

The declarative way is to set an **ambient group** with `@endnoteGroup(name)` on
a line of its own. Every note after it joins that group — you do not repeat the
group on each note — until the next `@endnoteGroup` changes it. Writing
`@endnoteGroup()` with empty parentheses resets to the default (ungrouped):

```
@endnoteGroup(chapter1)

Text with a note[fn: joins chapter1] and another[fn: also chapter1].

@endnoteGroup(chapter2)

More text[fn: joins chapter2].

@endnoteGroup()

Back to ungrouped notes[fn: in the default group].
```

The `@endnoteGroup` line produces no output; it simply changes the group that
following notes fall into. When both apply, a per-note `(group)` **overrides** the
ambient one, so you can drop a single note into another group without disturbing
the surrounding `@endnoteGroup`:

```
@endnoteGroup(main)

A main note[fn: goes to "main"], but this one[fn(aside): jumps to "aside"]
does not.
```

## Placing a group's list

A group's notes do not appear until you place them, with an `@endnotes` marker on
its own line. Name a group to place just that group:

```
@endnotes(refs)
```

or write a bare `@endnotes` to place **every** group that no named `@endnotes`
has already claimed:

```
@endnotes
```

Either form takes an optional `{title="…"}` attribute, which becomes a heading
above the list. Without it the list has no heading, so you are free to write your
own with an ordinary Markdown heading just before the marker:

```
## Notes to the Introduction

@endnotes(intro){title="Notes to the Introduction"}
```

Because placement is resolved after the whole document is parsed, an `@endnotes`
marker may appear **before** the notes it collects — handy when you want a
section's note list at the section's *start*, or a running list that gathers notes
introduced later.

## Numbering restarts for each group

Each group numbers its notes independently, restarting at **1**. This is the
convention readers expect from per-section or per-chapter endnotes: the third note
of Chapter 2 is "3", not "17". The superscript reference in the text carries the
per-group number, and the placed list matches it.

One consequence is worth stating plainly. Because numbering restarts, a **bare**
`@endnotes` that gathers several groups cannot show them as one flat list — two
groups would each contain a "1". So a bare `@endnotes` renders **one list per
group**: the default (ungrouped) group first with no label, then each named group
under its own `<h2 class="endnote-group-heading">` so the numbering is never
ambiguous. A single named `@endnotes(name)` needs no such label and gets none.

## Notes you do not place

Nothing is ever silently dropped. If a group has notes but no `@endnotes` marker
ever places it, its list is appended at the end of the document (just like the
historical single-section behaviour) and a build warning names the unplaced group.
Conversely, an `@endnotes(name)` for a group that has no notes renders nothing and
warns. Both warnings surface on stderr at the end of the build and, in watch mode,
as the amber banner.

## A worked example

The source below groups notes two ways — an ambient `@endnoteGroup` for the body
of each section, and a per-note override — then places each list under its own
heading:

```
@endnoteGroup(intro)

The framework rests on an idealisation[fn: agents are perfectly rational]
that the literature treats as harmless[fn: though see Simon 1955].

@endnoteGroup(methods)

We estimate the model by maximum likelihood[fn: full derivation in the
appendix], with one robustness check[fn(data): the 2019 sample is excluded].

## Notes — Introduction

@endnotes(intro){title="Notes — Introduction"}

## Notes — Methods

@endnotes(methods){title="Notes — Methods"}

## Data notes

@endnotes(data){title="Data notes"}
```

The two introduction notes number 1–2 under "Notes — Introduction"; the two
methods notes number 1–2 under "Notes — Methods"; and the single `(data)`
override note is a fresh "1" under "Data notes".

## Seeing it in action

The passage that follows contains real grouped notes — two in a `principles`
group and one in a `sources` group — and the two lists below it are placed live
on this page, each under its own title.

> Cooperation can be rational even among self-interested
> agents[^p1(principles): given the right correlation between their choices].
> The classic treatment predicts defection[^p2(principles): in the one-shot
> game with common knowledge of rationality], a result later editions
> revisit[^s1(sources): see the 2018 revision, chapter 4].

### Notes on the principles

@endnotes(principles){title="Notes on the principles"}

### Sources

@endnotes(sources){title="Sources"}

Notice that each list numbers from 1 independently, and that the superscripts in
the quotation point into the matching list.

## LaTeX output

Grouped endnotes are, by nature, an arrangement of collected notes — a shape that
plain page footnotes do not have. So the LaTeX behaviour follows what the document
actually asks for:

- If the document uses **no** grouping or placement at all, every note renders as
  an ordinary inline `\footnote{…}`, exactly as on the [Footnotes](footnotes.html)
  page. Nothing changes for existing documents.
- As soon as any grouping or `@endnotes` placement appears, notes become
  **endnotes**: the reference is a `\textsuperscript{n}` mark, and each `@endnotes`
  marker emits that group's list — an optional bold title, then the numbered note
  bodies, with a bold sub-heading per group under a bare `@endnotes`. JMarkdown
  owns the numbering, so the print output matches the web without relying on any
  particular LaTeX endnotes package.

## Reference

### Syntax

| Form | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[fn(g): body]` | Anonymous note in group `g` |
| `[^label(g): body]` | Labelled note in group `g` |
| `@endnoteGroup(name)` | Set the ambient group for following notes |
| `@endnoteGroup()` | Reset the ambient group to the default |
| `@endnotes(name)` | Place group `name`'s list here |
| `@endnotes` | Place every group not claimed by a named `@endnotes` |
| `@endnotes(name){title="…"}` | Place a list with a heading |

Group precedence, most specific first: a per-note `(group)`, then the ambient
`@endnoteGroup`, then the default (ungrouped) group.

### HTML classes and ids

| Selector | Where it appears |
|---|---|
| `sup.footnote-ref` | The superscript reference marker |
| `section.footnotes` | A placed (or trailing) endnote list |
| `section.footnotes[data-endnote-group]` | A single-group list, carrying its group name |
| `h1.endnotes-heading` | The `{title="…"}` heading of a list |
| `h2.endnote-group-heading` | A group's sub-heading within a bare `@endnotes` list |
| `li#fn-LABEL` | One note in the list |
| `a.footnote-backref` | The `↩` link from a note back to its reference |

JMarkdown ships no default styling for these classes; supply the CSS in your
template or stylesheet.

